From 701caa43da05054bad0ca698eefc767555a5c517 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:29:49 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 52/52] client: real ECH in a Chrome-shaped hello, as BoringSSL
 sends it

With chrome_hello set, ECH was only ever a GREASE extension. A config may
now offer real ECH (ClientConfig::set_ech_mode, for a config list that
comes from a DNS lookup per server), and both hellos are BoringSSL's,
measured against Chrome 152 with a local ECH server:

- the outer hello is Chrome's usual one with the public name in SNI and the
  real ECH in place of the GREASE one; it still offers TLS 1.2, as Chrome's
  does (with_ech restricts a whole config to TLS 1.3), and a server that
  answers with TLS 1.2 has rejected the offer;
- the inner hello has the same GREASE values and extension order, TLS 1.3
  suites and version alone, no renegotiation_info, extended_master_secret,
  session_ticket or ec_point_formats, the ECH marker, the real SNI; the
  ECH marker, supported_versions and SNI come first and every other
  extension follows in the outer hello's order, sent as one
  ech_outer_extensions, so the payload is BoringSSL's size (144 bytes for a
  21-byte name where the whole inner hello took 1510);
- the inner hello is padded as BoringSSL pads it (a maximum_name_length of
  0 means none, where rustls padded to 255).

A server that rejects the offer is verified as the config's public name
(RFC 9849 6.1.7; rustls checked the inner name, so a rejection ended in a
certificate error instead of the rejection and its retry configs), and
EchConfig::from_retry_configs turns the configs it sent into an EchConfig
for the one retry a client makes.
